    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d28">

                <pb facs="0135" n="43" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp01v01/pg43"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72119/1166</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Assistant Secretary of State</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Phillips</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the French
                        Ambassador</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Jusserand</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-01-26">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">January 26, 1918</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. Ambassador</hi>: Referring to the
                    inquiry which Mr. Sartiges of your Embassy telephoned to my
                    office a few days ago, regarding the interpretation of item number 10 in the
                    peace program outlined by the President in his address to Congress on January 8,
                    I hasten to confirm the telephone message with Mr. Sartiges
                    this morning to the effect that the President has replied to an inquiry
                    submitted to him that the statement meant “that we wish the place of the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">peoples</hi> of Austria-Hungary safeguarded and
                    assured.”</p>
                <p>I am [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">William Phillips</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
